For compilation, just type make.
Installation: make install.

There is no ./configure.  The top-level Makefile descends into lib/, and
lib/Makefile runs lib/configure on every build, so the environment of the
make is what decides.  make install depends on all, so one command does
both.

On linux, hpux, etc. wampes goes to /tcp (historic reasons).
On macos it goes to /usr/local/wampes

If you like to install it to another path.
/tcp is not linux FHS conform. You may like to install it to
/var/wampes or /var/ax25/wampes or /opt/wampes
	TCPDIR=/somewhere make install

These four are remembered in lib/configure.mak, so a setting only has to be
made once, and make clean keeps that file - otherwise the one command you
run between trying a change and keeping it would throw the setting away.
Two things are deliberately not remembered: a TCPDIR that is just /tcp or
/usr/local/wampes is the platform default rather than a choice, and a
BINDIR or SBINDIR that is only $TCPDIR/bin or $TCPDIR/sbin goes when
TCPDIR goes.

To go back to the platform defaults: make distclean

doc/INSTALL-PATHS.md has the whole layout, including where each of these
four settings ends up.

For connectivity to libax25, adjust your wampes.conf in /etc/ax25
or /usr/local/etc/ax25.
If you compile libax25, tell it where this WAMPES will live, so that the
example line in the wampes.conf it installs is right.  In the libax25
source directory:

	./configure --with-wampes-tcpdir=/somewhere

-> Then the example line in the wampes.conf that libax25 installs points
at $TCPDIR/sockets/ax25.  It is left commented out either way, because
libax25 cannot ask an installed WAMPES what TCPDIR it was built with.
Uncomment it, or leave it alone if the default was right.
